#: ../../source/fs-extra_README.md:250
msgid "Naming"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/fs-extra_README.md:253
msgid ""
"I put a lot of thought into the naming of these functions. Inspired by "
"@coolaj86's request. So he deserves much of the credit for raising the "
"issue. See discussion(s) here:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/fs-extra_README.md:255
msgid "https://github.com/jprichardson/node-fs-extra/issues/2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/fs-extra_README.md:256
msgid "https://github.com/flatiron/utile/issues/11"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/fs-extra_README.md:257
msgid "https://github.com/ryanmcgrath/wrench-js/issues/29"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/fs-extra_README.md:258
msgid "https://github.com/substack/node-mkdirp/issues/17"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/fs-extra_README.md:260
msgid ""
"First, I believe that in as many cases as possible, the [Node.js naming "
"schemes](http://nodejs.org/api/fs.html) should be chosen. However, there "
"are problems with the Node.js own naming schemes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/fs-extra_README.md:262
msgid ""
"For example, `fs.readFile()` and `fs.readdir()`: the **F** is capitalized"
" in *File* and the **d** is not capitalized in *dir*. Perhaps a bit "
"pedantic, but they should still be consistent. Also, Node.js has chosen a"
" lot of POSIX naming schemes, which I believe is great. See: "
"`fs.mkdir()`, `fs.rmdir()`, `fs.chown()`, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/fs-extra_README.md:264
msgid ""
"We have a dilemma though. How do you consistently name methods that "
"perform the following POSIX commands: `cp`, `cp -r`, `mkdir -p`, and `rm "
"-rf`?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/fs-extra_README.md:266
msgid ""
"My perspective: when in doubt, err on the side of simplicity. A directory"
" is just a hierarchical grouping of directories and files. Consider that "
"for a moment. So when you want to copy it or remove it, in most cases "
"you'll want to copy or remove all of its contents. When you want to "
"create a directory, if the directory that it's suppose to be contained in"
" does not exist, then in most cases you'll want to create that too."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/fs-extra_README.md:268
msgid ""
"So, if you want to remove a file or a directory regardless of whether it "
"has contents, just call `fs.remove(path)`. If you want to copy a file or "
"a directory whether it has contents, just call `fs.copy(source, "
"destination)`. If you want to create a directory regardless of whether "
"its parent directories exist, just call `fs.mkdirs(path)` or "
"`fs.mkdirp(path)`."
msgstr ""

